WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:09.240
Democrats and Republicans alike have
jumped on the "break 'em up" bandwagon,

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:13.280
embracing antitrust solutions to rein in
the behavior of technology giants.

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:17.040
These would-be trust-busters claim that Big
Tech companies like Google, Amazon,

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:22.500
and Facebook crush startups and shut out
innovation. But do these activists need a reality check?

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:26.220
For instance, Amazon
controls just 5% of the US retail market

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:29.980
and Amazon also does exactly what
Democrats like Bernie Sanders and

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:34.340
Chuck Schumer want Corporate America
to do more of: foregoing stock buybacks

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:39.780
to invest massively in R &amp; D. And it's not just
Amazon — the top five US corporate R &amp; D investors

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:43.530
are all Big Tech companies. But ask
yourself — would these companies spend

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:48.180
nearly as much if they had to shut down or
split off as much as half of their business?

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:52.649
And might banning new Big Tech
acquisitions deny a crucial off-ramp to

00:00:52.649 --> 00:00:57.140
those who invest early in promising
startups? With all public policy decisions,

00:00:57.140 --> 00:01:00.540
there are trade-offs and
unintended consequences to consider.

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:04.000
When it comes to breaking up Big Tech,
we risk stifling America's most

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:06.820
innovative and promising sector.

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:09.460
What do you think about breaking up Big Tech?

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:12.780
Let us know in our poll. Also, let us know
what other topics you'd like our

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:16.860
scholars to cover in 60 seconds and be
sure to LIKE and subscribe for more

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:20.450
research and videos from AEI.

